$DIR/main.p:30:17: cannot range over t (variable of struct type table): table has no method All returning an iterator
    A for range loop over a value that is not a string, integer, array, slice,
    map, channel or function calls the value's All method and ranges over the
    iterator it returns, an iter.Seq or iter.Seq2. This type has no All method.
    Give the type an All method that returns an iterator, or range over one of
    its other iterators.
    Library containers such as vec.Vec and hash.Map are structs, not built-in
    types, so the loop asks them for an iterator by name. vec.Vec and hash.Map
    have All, so they range like Go's slices and maps.
    Add an All method:
        func (t *T) All() iter.Seq2[string, int] {
            return func(yield func(string, int) bool) {
                // call yield for each key and value
            }
        }
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2456" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:34:17: cannot range over l: method All of list must have the form All() iter.Seq[V] or All() iter.Seq2[K, V]
    A for range loop over a container calls its All method, which must take no
    arguments and return an iterator: a function of the form func(yield func(K,
    V) bool), such as iter.Seq[V] or iter.Seq2[K, V]. This type's All method has
    another form.
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2458" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:37:17: cannot range over get(): method All of seq has a pointer receiver, and get() is not addressable
    A for range loop over a container calls its All method. That method has a
    pointer receiver, so the loop needs the address of the container. Here the
    container is a value that has no address, such as the result of a function
    call.
    Store the container in a variable first, or range over a pointer to it.
    Store it in a variable first:
        m := load()
        for k, v := range m {
            use(k, v)
        }
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2459" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:40:20: cannot range over load(): method All of hash.Map[string, int] has a pointer receiver, and load() is not addressable
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2459" for more)
